  {
    "number": 110,
    "title": "Share SSE client delivery while preserving feed policies (#81)",
    "body": "Refs #81. Delivered after #80's verified main revision `979bd73d9385f46f8e3bf4d4d8d1e1fd26c6740a`.\n\nEvents and ControllerEvents now use one private SseDelivery for encoded-message queues, authentication, heartbeat/drain timers, backpressure and cleanup. The feed owners retain snapshot signatures, history, cursor namespaces/parsing, replay/resync decisions and wire envelopes. Public authentication wiring and HTTP security admission are unchanged.\n\nCharacterization recorded two deliberate policies: authenticated browser delivery checks blocking after authorization and allows drain during that wait; native delivery rejects enqueue while blocked and terminates immediately when a false write leaves queued messages. Unauthenticated browser delivery remains synchronous. No callback is added to admission-only feeds.\n\nOwnership and recovery walkthrough:\n- HTTP security still owns the global 16 mixed-feed/HEAD streams and 32 requests. Existing feed-local pools remain bounded. Native registration reserves before catalog/authentication waits, and delivery activates only after SSE headers.\n- Authenticated queues retain 32 entries including the message awaiting auth. Rechecks remain one second, heartbeats 15 seconds, and drain ceilings five seconds. Native rechecks may close blocked clients earlier, as before.\n- Rejection, disconnect and shutdown discard queued data and release delivery resources. Late authorization cannot write for removed clients. Drain listeners are removed during cleanup.\n- Publication never awaits a client. Reconnection uses the original feed replay/resync rules and never executes commands, stops playback or refreshes physical evidence. Existing player/device writer and selected shared-state owner are untouched.\n- Native close-listener attachment remains at activation after registration awaits; this extraction does not redesign that existing registration edge.\n\nADR 0023 and the archived issue-linked OpenSpec change describe the boundary. No product specification delta applies (`skip_specs: true`); package versions, public schemas and persistence are unchanged. Installation, live-state changes and device operations are excluded.\n\nFrozen comparison: base/merge-base `979bd73d9385f46f8e3bf4d4d8d1e1fd26c6740a`; head `15fc99d3fd6afd1147b310829ac0d08b72d8b403`. Review command: `git diff 979bd73d9385f46f8e3bf4d4d8d1e1fd26c6740a...15fc99d3fd6afd1147b310829ac0d08b72d8b403`. One commit; clean worktree.\n\nLocal Node 24 evidence: `npm ci`; 23 baseline lifecycle cases on unchanged feed source; `npm run check` (671 tests, including 27 delivery cases, 19 current specs, 10 workflow fixtures); `npm run test:controller` (239 tests); Chromium install and `npm run test:browser` (68 scenarios); both workflow checks repeated after archive; `git diff --check`. No existing tests were removed or weakened. The source extraction's independent implementation check found no blocking regression; final frozen-head reviews and all three configured hosted jobs remain pending.\n\nFresh independent Standards and Specification reviews of `15fc99d3fd6afd1147b310829ac0d08b72d8b403` against `979bd73d9385f46f8e3bf4d4d8d1e1fd26c6740a` returned no findings. Both verified a clean, nonempty frozen comparison and inspected the relevant ownership/authentication/concurrency contracts. The Specification reviewer independently ran all 27 lifecycle tests successfully. Neither changed source or GitHub. GitHub reviews and inline threads were empty on readback.\n",
    "state": "open",
